#Sim Playtest

A fuzz test, not a proof: a generic player policy (needing no per-map
authorship) chooses moves turn by turn against a seeded random ghost,
repeated across many seeds, tallying how often the game is actually won.
This answers "is this map winnable" the same way manual playtesting does,
but repeatably, in bulk, and without a human needing to sit down and try.

A hand-written fixed move sequence was tried first and dropped: it desyncs
silently after a mid-script reset (the script's position no longer matches
where the reset actually left the game), which showed up as a permanently
stuck "incomplete" result no turn budget could fix. A policy sidesteps
this by construction — it's handed the actual current state fresh every
turn, so there's no script position to go stale. It also scales to new
maps for free, since nothing about it is written with any particular
map's topology in mind.

A marking is just a snapshot of where every token currently is, so a
simulated playout can equally well start from the map's normal initial
state or from any specific, hand-picked marking worth targeting — a
dangerous moment found during manual or fuzz-tested play, reproduced
exactly rather than hoped into existence by a long move sequence. This is
the "icing" layer: once general fuzzing has found or suggested an
interesting state, a trial can be seeded to start from exactly there.

    function makeRng(seed: number): () => number {
      let s = seed >>> 0;
      return function (): number {
        s |= 0; s = (s + 0x6D2B79F5) | 0;
        let t = Math.imul(s ^ (s >>> 15), 1 | s);
        t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t;
        return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;
      };
    }

##Player Policies

A policy only ever sees the current net, the current chomper marking, and
which dots remain — never anything about which map it's on — so the same
two policies run unmodified against any GameNet.

randomPolicy picks uniformly among whatever's currently enabled, the same
rule the ghost itself follows. It needs no notion of progress at all, so a
low win rate from it doesn't distinguish "the map is hard" from "blind
luck is bad at joins" — it's a floor, not a fair assessment on its own.

greedyPolicy is a simple-but-competent baseline: multi-source breadth
first search, starting from every place that still has a dot and walking
arcs backwards, labels every place with its distance to the nearest dot.
Each enabled transition is scored by the best (lowest) label among its
output places, and the policy fires whichever enabled transition gets
some token closest to a dot, breaking ties on the seeded rng. It has no
idea a join needs two arms coordinated or that a corridor leads to a
trap — it only ever asks "what's closest" — so losing to a map despite
playing greedily is a meaningfully stronger signal than losing to random
chance.

    type PlayerPolicy = (
      net: PetriNet, chompers: Marking, dots: Set<string>,
      rng: () => number,
    ) => string | null;

    function randomPolicy(
      net: PetriNet, chompers: Marking, _dots: Set<string>, rng: () => number,
    ): string | null {
      const enabled = enabledTransitions(net, chompers);
      if (enabled.length === 0) return null;
      return enabled[Math.floor(rng() * enabled.length)];
    }

    function nearestDotDistances(
      net: PetriNet, dots: Set<string>,
    ): Map<string, number> {
      const dist = new Map<string, number>();
      const queue: string[] = [];
      for (const d of dots) { dist.set(d, 0); queue.push(d); }
      let head = 0;
      while (head < queue.length) {
        const cur = queue[head++];
        const d = dist.get(cur)!;
        for (const arc of net.arcs) {
          if (arc.target === cur && !dist.has(arc.source)) {
            dist.set(arc.source, d + 1);
            queue.push(arc.source);
          }
        }
      }
      return dist;
    }

    function greedyPolicy(
      net: PetriNet, chompers: Marking, dots: Set<string>, rng: () => number,
    ): string | null {
      const enabled = enabledTransitions(net, chompers);
      if (enabled.length === 0) return null;
      if (dots.size === 0) return enabled[0];

      const dist = nearestDotDistances(net, dots);
      let best: string[] = [];
      let bestScore = Infinity;
      for (const t of enabled) {
        const outs = outputArcs(net, t).map(a => a.target);
        const score = Math.min(...outs.map(p => dist.get(p) ?? Infinity));
        if (score < bestScore) { bestScore = score; best = [t]; }
        else if (score === bestScore) { best.push(t); }
      }
      return best[Math.floor(rng() * best.length)];
    }

##Running One Playthrough

A policy is asked fresh every turn, from whatever the actual current state
is — there is no script position that can go stale, so a reset mid-game
is just another state the policy reacts to, not a special case.

A start override lets a trial begin from a specific hand-picked marking
instead of the map's normal initial one, for targeting a known-dangerous
state directly rather than hoping a long playout wanders into it.

    type Outcome = 'won' | 'lost' | 'incomplete';

    interface StartOverride {
      chompers?: Array<[string, number]>;
      ghosts?: Array<[string, number]>;
    }

    function simulatePlayout(
      gn: GameNet, policy: PlayerPolicy, seed: number,
      maxTurns = 200, start?: StartOverride,
    ): Outcome {
      const engine = makeEngine(gn);
      if (start?.chompers) {
        engine.state.chompers = emptyMarking();
        for (const [p, c] of start.chompers) markingAddTokens(engine.state.chompers, p, c);
      }
      if (start?.ghosts) {
        engine.state.ghosts = emptyMarking();
        for (const [p, c] of start.ghosts) markingAddTokens(engine.state.ghosts, p, c);
      }
      const rng = makeRng(seed);

      for (let turn = 0; turn < maxTurns; turn++) {
        if (isWon(engine.state)) return 'won';
        const move = policy(engine.state.gn.net, engine.state.chompers, engine.state.dots, rng);
        if (move === null) return 'incomplete';

        playerMove(engine, move);
        if (engine.phase === 'lost') return 'lost';
        if (isWon(engine.state)) return 'won';

        let guard = 0;
        while (engine.phase === 'ghost' && guard++ < 50) {
          ghostTurn(engine, rng);
          if (engine.phase === 'lost') return 'lost';
        }
      }
      return isWon(engine.state) ? 'won' : 'incomplete';
    }

##Running Many Seeds

    interface TrialResult {
      won: number;
      lost: number;
      incomplete: number;
      total: number;
    }

    function runTrials(
      gnFactory: () => GameNet, policy: PlayerPolicy,
      nTrials: number, baseSeed = 1, maxTurns = 200, start?: StartOverride,
    ): TrialResult {
      const result: TrialResult = { won: 0, lost: 0, incomplete: 0, total: nTrials };
      for (let i = 0; i < nTrials; i++) {
        const outcome = simulatePlayout(gnFactory(), policy, baseSeed + i, maxTurns, start);
        result[outcome]++;
      }
      return result;
    }

##Diamond: proving out the generic policies

A hand-written fixed sequence had reported Diamond as unwinnable (0 wins
in 200 seeded trials) — wrongly, as it turned out. The generic policies
disagree sharply: randomPolicy wins 25% of the time, greedyPolicy wins
about 75%. The fixed script's result was an artifact of its own rigidity,
not a property of the map — see #Game Map's Diamond section for the
mechanism (dots persist across a life-loss reset, so Diamond rewards
incremental progress across several cheap attempts rather than requiring
one clean traversal, which a script with no notion of "good enough for
now" can't exploit).

This is also the reason Diamond is a good first target for the generic
policies specifically: the prior (wrong) answer was already on record, so
disagreement here was a legible signal that the new harness — not the
map — needed a closer look, rather than something that could pass
unnoticed.

##Mandala: a structural sanity check, not a fuzz test

This hand-written sequence is kept only to check the win condition itself
is reachable at all — a mechanism check, not a survival-against-the-ghost
one (that's what the generic policies are for). Reaching Goal is not the
same as winning — isWon checks every dot on the map is gone, and the
straight hub-to-goal cascade never visits Mid, which has its own dot. A
sequence that actually wins has to detour one corridor through an escape
to Mid and back, then re-fork that arm to rebuild it.

    const MANDALA_WIN_MOVES = [
      't_fork0', 't_fork_n1', 't_fork_n2',
      't_nw_c', 't_sw_c', 't_ne_c', 't_se_c',
      't_nwc_esc', 't_mid_home', 't_join_e',
      't_fork0', 't_fork_n1', 't_nw_c', 't_join_w', 't_join',
    ];

Checked separately from the seeded trials, with the ghost turn skipped
entirely: this isolates "does the sequence itself reach isWon" from "does
it survive the ghost," so a failure here points at the sequence being
wrong rather than at bad luck.

    function _mandalaMovesWinWithoutGhost(): boolean {
      const engine = makeEngine(makeMandalaMap());
      engine.state.ghosts = emptyMarking();
      for (const move of MANDALA_WIN_MOVES) {
        playerMove(engine, move);
        if (engine.phase === 'ghost') engine.phase = 'player';
      }
      return isWon(engine.state);
    }

~run
    const diamondRandom = runTrials(makeDiamondMap, randomPolicy, 200);
    console.log('Diamond, random policy, 200 seeds:', JSON.stringify(diamondRandom));

    const diamondGreedy = runTrials(makeDiamondMap, greedyPolicy, 200);
    console.log('Diamond, greedy policy, 200 seeds:', JSON.stringify(diamondGreedy));

---

#Tests

##diamond is winnable — a fixed script had wrongly reported otherwise
    runTrials(makeDiamondMap, randomPolicy, 200).won >= 20
    runTrials(makeDiamondMap, greedyPolicy, 200).won >= 100

##mandala win sequence satisfies isWon with no ghost interference
    _mandalaMovesWinWithoutGhost()

